 {
  "id": "G342",
  "article_id": "gnews:56085a9d3e88c464bcaed67b",
  "title": "Develop Humanoid Robot Policies End-to-End with NVIDIA Isaac GR00T | NVIDIA Technical Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "By Edith Llontop and Brandon Neel\nThe NVIDIA Isaac GR00T Development Platform unifies every stage of the humanoid robot pipeline, from data collection and model training to large-scale evaluation and deployment readiness.\nGR00T 1.7 is the first open, commercially usable vision-language-action model for generalized humanoid skills, released under the Apache 2.0 license with a 3-billion-parameter base checkpoint.\nThe model introduces a Cosmos-Reason2-2B (Qwen3-VL architecture) backbone, robust pre",
  "passage": "…ur own robot setup, define a task for your use case, collect demonstrations on your system, and reuse the same data conversion, post-training, and evaluation workflow.\nA growing GR00T ecosystem\nThe GR00T platform and reference workflow are already being adopted across a growing ecosystem of humanoid robotics partners.\nHumanoid makers and AI providers including 1X, Agility, ANYBotics, Bellboy Robotics, FieldAI, Lightwheel AI, NEURA Robotics, Nexuni, Noble Machines, Schaeffler, Skild AI, and Techman Robot are integrating components of the GR00T platform such as Isaac Teleop, Sim, Lab, and ROS to accelerate their development pipeline and bring AI-enabled robots into real industrial use faster.\nResearch institutions including Stanford, CMU, UCSD, ETH Zurich, and AI2 will be experimenting with the unified GR00T end-to-end workflow to reduce integration complexity and move faster from robot bring-up to skill development and real-world validation.\nLeading wearable and XR device makers including Haptikos, Manus, Noitom, OpenGraph Labs, PICO, Sensing, HTC Vive, Wuji, and Xsens offer devices with native support for NVIDIA Isaac Teleop, making it easier to capture high-quality demonstrations.\nThe GR00T platform and GR00T 1.7 model are available today for any developer to use. Whether starting in simulation or on a physical robot, the workflow remains the same. Select an entry point, define a bounded task, and build your first end-to-end policy.\nGetting started can be challenging, so we have built a complete end-to-end refe…",
  "statement": "Research institutions including Stanford, CMU, UCSD, ETH Zurich, and AI2 will be experimenting with the unified GR00T end-to-end workflow"
 },
 {
  "id": "G223",
  "article_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6",
  "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Up until now the majority of AI buildouts have been primarily cashflow funded by the hyperscalers such as Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, Oracle. Over the last year, that's started to turn with Oracle then Meta, and now even Google turning to debt. Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accele",
  "passage": "…t grade credit rating and are satisfied to lend matching the length of the backstop. With a viable go to market plan and an Nvidia backstop, equity can be raised to fund deposits to secure equipment and the various payments needed to start developing the cluster.\nOfftake: Having the backstop in hand and with funding for the cluster, Neoclouds can then tap previously unaddressed offtake demand outside of just the typical 5-year AI lab or hyperscale offtake.\nDatacenter: However, even with the backstop in place, securing the final leg of the Trinity and obtaining datacenter capacity remains challenging absent a creative datacenter rental structure or resorting to a self-built datacenter. Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\nThe incremental revenue from the backstop program will be significant, but Nvidia stands to gain far more from these backstops than this additional revenue. They aim to do nothing less than entirely reshape the structure of the GPU market itself. We have already discussed how the TAM they can sell into will become bottlenecked very soon if a 5y hyperscaler backstopped offtake is the only viable deal structure.\nIn January 2026 for institutional subscribers, we first discussed how Nvidia was becoming the Central Bank of AI . A central bank exists to supply liquidity when others in the banking system are unwilling to step in, supporting economic activity until others are ready to take over.\nM…",
  "statement": "Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases"
 },
 {
  "id": "G126",
  "article_id": "gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1",
  "title": "Here's What a $25,000 Investment in Nvidia Could Be Worth if History Repeats Itself",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "The artificial intelligence chip giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) has struggled recently and is now down nearly 13% over the past month (as of July 1). After years of an incredible AI rally, doubts are now creeping into investors' minds.\nThere are questions about whether the large tech companies can continue to invest hundreds of billions annually in AI infrastructure, which is driving much of the cycle, and whether constraints such as energy and power will eventually have a real impact. For Nvidia ",
  "passage": "…impact. For Nvidia specifically, competition in the chip space seems to be intensifying.\nThat said, Nvidia hasn't done anything to suggest its business is at risk of slowing, so if the concerns don't materialize, the stock could have upside. Here's what a $25,000 investment in Nvidia could be worth if history repeats itself.\nWhile many high-flying AI companies trade at monster valuations, that's not really something you can say about Nvidia.\nOn the surface, Nvidia trading at roughly 22 times forward earnings looks quite reasonable. In the first quarter of Nvidia's fiscal year 2027, ending April 26, the company grew revenue 85% year over year, while diluted earnings per share soared by 140%.\nIn March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\nFurthermore, in its first fiscal quarter of 2027, Nvidia announced plans to become much more involved in the central processing unit (CPU) space , which has been in high demand due to the rise of agentic AI. Management said they think they can generate nearly $20 billion of CPU sales, making it an instant industry leader.\nObviously, there are real concerns about Nvidia from an industry perspective and a company-specific perspective.\nFrom an industry perspective, if AI experiences a significant pullback or turns out not to be as consequential as promised, that would significantly impact Nvidia, which is at the center of the AI trade. A pullback could occur if hyperscalers slow capital expen…",
  "statement": "In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027"
 },
 {
  "id": "G184",
  "article_id": "gnews:4a892d75e355bee00aa3c572",
  "title": "Nvidia vs. Palantir: Which Artificial Intelligence (AI) Stock Is the Better Buy Right Now?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "The artificial intelligence (AI) investment opportunity has become more measured throughout 2026. After years of parabolic gains driven by explosive demand for generative models and the underlying infrastructure that supports them, both Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) and Palantir Technologies ( PLTR +1.34% ) have been relatively subdued stocks so far this year.\nWith that said, I think the muted stock performance of Nvidia and Palantir reflects broader digestion of lofty expectations rather than a fundam",
  "passage": "…ild-out of AI capacity. Meanwhile, Palantir's business is software-led and largely dependent on organizations embedding its tools into operational workflows. While both trajectories remain positive, Nvidia's scale and profit margin profile give it higher operating leverage and financial flexibility to explore new opportunities.\nValuation is where the investment prospects between Nvidia and Palantir really start to diverge. Specifically, the forward price-to-earnings (P/E) multiple shows a notable difference in how the market is pricing each company.\nInvestors will notice that both Nvidia and Palantir have experienced meaningful compression in their valuation profiles this year. But even so, Nvidia's forward P/E ratio of 22 is just one-quarter that of Palantir's. This is an important concept to understand. Even though Nvidia's market capitalization of $4.7 trillion is nearly 15x Palantir's, it is the cheaper stock by far.\nThis begs the question: Why is there such a large disparity between Nvidia and Palantir?\nFor Palantir, the central growth narrative revolves around accelerating the adoption of AI agents and autonomous workflows. Palantir AIP is designed to help companies build and deploy custom applications on top of their existing data silos -- transforming how large organizations operate.\nWhile this thesis is compelling in principle, translating it into sustained revenue and profit growth requires overcoming integration complexity with existing…",
  "statement": "Nvidia's forward P/E ratio of 22 is just one-quarter that of Palantir's"
 },
 {
  "id": "G505",
  "article_id": "gnews:488fd6e4ba9177df77a2fed2",
  "title": "SpaceX Wants to Borrow $40 Billion to Buy Nvidia Chips",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-07",
  "article_opening": "SpaceX is borrowing $40 billion for NVIDIA chips while posting a $541 million quarterly loss on capex more than double its revenue.\nApollo Global Management is expected to lead the deal, as Morgan Stanley warns AI infrastructure will need $1.5 trillion in outside financing by 2028.\nLockup expirations will release up to 1.3 billion SpaceX shares after Q3 earnings, pressuring stock as insiders sell right after a post-IPO rally.\nA recent report citing people familiar with the matter said SpaceX ( N",
  "passage": "…oans are the easier part, because a small group of lenders can agree on terms quickly, but selling bonds that large to a wide investor base requires work with rating agencies and a marketing process that takes quarters.\nApollo is expected to lead the deal and place pieces with other buyers. Pimco is among the lenders in talks.\nThe financing is expected to close in 2027, which means the terms, the lender group and the final size could all still change before any money moves.\nIn the second quarter of 2026, SpaceX reported revenue of $7.81 billion against capital spending of $18.37 billion, of which $15.83 billion went to AI. It posted a net loss of $541 million.\nOn the August 4 earnings call, management said the next two quarters should look \"very similar\" on spending, and Elon Musk said, \"So we're exclusive to NVIDIA.\"\nBorrowing avoids diluting shareholders today. Even so, lockup expirations will add 328.4 million marketable shares on October 9, another 328.4 million on October 24 and up to 1.3 billion after third-quarter earnings.\nThe stock priced at $135 in its June 11, 2026 initial public offering, peaked at $225.64 and closed at $171.92 on October 6. That close came one session after a 7.63% gain to its highest close since June, so insiders got their first chance to sell right after a rally, as the debt was being marketed.\nNVIDIA closed at $239.24 after a record intraday high of $243.37, worth about $5.78 trillion. A large order matters, but at that size, no single customer changes the business.\nMorga…",
  "statement": "management said the next two quarters should look \"very similar\" on spending, and Elon Musk said, \"So we're exclusive to NVIDIA.\""
 },
 {
  "id": "G155",
  "article_id": "gnews:2934d9634471f3560f8697e0",
  "title": "Nvidia Stock Is Down 13% Over the Last Month. Here's Why That Could Be Good News.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-05",
  "article_opening": "It's never fun to see your investments lose money. And when a stock's price falls more than 10% in a month, it's normal to wonder if you should sell to lock in your gains before it falls any further.\nThat's something investors in Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) need to seriously think about. Shares of the world's leading chipmaker dropped 12.6% between June 2 and July 2, and they're down 17% from May's all-time high of $235.74/share.\nBut this latest drop might not be such a bad thing, actually. Here's wh",
  "passage": "…7% between March 30 and May 14, hitting all-time highs.\nWhen a stock rockets upward and never experiences temporary pullbacks like Nvidia's has over the last five years, it can make new investors reluctant to buy shares out of concern that the stock is too expensive. And as the world's largest company by market cap, Nvidia already faces skepticism about its valuation.\nIt can also set the company up to experience a major share price drop if it reports anything less than stellar earnings. Nvidia doesn't seem to have that problem. In fact, it almost faces the opposite issue: Over the past two years, Nvidia's earnings reports have been stellar, with massive growth in revenue and net income. Yet after five of its last eight earnings reports, the stock has seen an immediate and significant share price decline .\nIf recent history is any guide, Nvidia's recent share price drop is likely to reverse itself unexpectedly, and the stock should soar to new heights . Smart investors know better than to panic sell this longtime winner that's still at the top of its game.\nBy John Bromels – Jul 5, 2026 at 2:15PM EST",
  "statement": "after five of its last eight earnings reports, the stock has seen an immediate and significant share price decline"
 },
 {
  "id": "G228",
  "article_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6",
  "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Up until now the majority of AI buildouts have been primarily cashflow funded by the hyperscalers such as Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, Oracle. Over the last year, that's started to turn with Oracle then Meta, and now even Google turning to debt. Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accele",
  "passage": "…uctive output, and therefore the revenue-generating capacity, of a cluster they are financing. It is an important resource for mapping token demand into GPU demand.\nNvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia, and there are still others that are considering engaging with the backstop program. Let’s briefly discuss the deals that have been announced to date – all of which are in the Asia Pacific region so far.\nThe first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\nFirmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program. Firmus’ first clusters were based in Singapore and focused on H100s deployed usi…",
  "statement": "Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B"
 },
 {
  "id": "G310",
  "article_id": "gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250",
  "title": "Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Price change for Nvidia stock in the last 6 months: 4%\n$NVDA Share Price as of Jul. 2: $195\n52-Week High: $237\n$NVDA Stock Price Target: $302\nNvidia ( NVDA ) stock barely moved on Monday after news broke that one of the company’s biggest upcoming products is running late. Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year , from 2027 to 2028.\nKyber matters because it’s built to pack 144 of Nvidia’s most powerful chips int",
  "passage": "… power, but that idea was scrapped too.\nCloud customers reportedly rejected it because the design was awkward and the service was expensive to run. SemiAnalysis says this leaves Nvidia without a proven way to scale up Rubin Ultra for now, which could give rivals like AMD and Google an opening at the high end of the AI chip market. Nvidia has not responded to requests for comment.\nDespite this news, Nvidia stock isn’t showing much reaction, trading close to flat. That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\nSemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia’s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations.\nNvidia’s recent earnings back up why investors aren’t panicking.\nIn the most recent quarter, the company posted $82 billion in total revenue, up 85% year-over-year, with data center revenue alone hitting $75 billion.\nFree cash flow came in at a record $49 billion.\nCEO Jensen Huang has repeatedly said demand for AI infrastructure remains “parabolic,” driven by hyperscalers, AI-native clouds, and now a growing CPU business built around the new Vera chip.\nSee analysts’ growth forecasts and price targets for Nvidia stock (It’s free) >>>\nWhat the Market Is Telling Us About Nvidia Stock\nThe muted stock reaction suggests investors see this delay as a bump in the road rather than a major setback.\nN…",
  "statement": "SemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia’s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations."
 },
 {
  "id": "G526",
  "article_id": "gnews:b5615714b8f92f78c23233c7",
  "title": "Apple, Nvidia lead list of tech companies that donated to Trump’s White House ballroom project",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-16",
  "article_opening": "Some of the biggest tech companies donated tens of millions of dollars for the construction of the White House ballroom.\nBy Matt Binder\nMatt Binder joined Mashable's tech vertical in 2018, where he covers social media, tech policy, cybersecurity, online scams, cryptocurrency, AI, creator news, weird tech, and other related tech beats.\nHere are the tech companies that gave millions to Donald Trump's White House ballroom – and just how much they donated..\nSome of the world's biggest tech companies",
  "passage": "…now we know exactly how much money they gave to the White House, too.\nAccording to a new report from the Washington Post , Apple and Nvidia topped the list of donors to Trump's ballroom project, giving $25 million each for the president's pet project. The two tech companies were among the top four donors overall, alongside the Adelson Family Foundation and Charles Ergen, the co-founder of Dish Network.\nHowever, Apple and Nvidia were far from the only tech companies to make massive donations for the ballroom.\nGoogle provided $22 million, via a settlement the company made with Trump related to the shutdown of his YouTube channel following the events of Jan. 6, as Mashable reported previously.\nMicrosoft donated $10 million, and Palantir was right behind them with a near $10 million donation of their own.\nAmazon, Meta, and T-Mobile donated $5 million each.\nA number of cryptocurrency companies like Coinbase, Ripple, and the Winklevoss twins, who represent their Gemini crypto exchange, also donated $5 million. Crypto exchange Kraken donated $1 million.\nHP and Micron each gave the president $2.5 million and Salesforce donated $2 million.\nNon-tech companies like defense contractors and the former CEO of Marvel's foundation also gave tens of millions of dollars to the ballroom project.\nThe voluntary accord lays out four layers of safety controls, while leaving companies to decide how and whether to enforce them.\nBy Chance Townsend\n\"Isn't this bullroom of yours supposed to include a bunch of top s…",
  "statement": "Microsoft donated $10 million, and Palantir was right behind them with a near $10 million donation of their own."
 },
 {
  "id": "G275",
  "article_id": "gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells. NVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18…\nBy Omor Ibne Ehsan\nNVIDIA posted 85% revenue growth to $82 billion in Q1, but half its data center sales come from hyperscalers actively funding custom AI chip alternatives.\nAmazon",
  "passage": "… billion buyback was authorized.\nAll of this says NVDA stock is set to keep delivering, as long as the broader market remains bullish.\nThe customer list is the threat. Amazon has disclosed Trainium is now a multi-billion-dollar business, and every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA’s data center segment also funds an alternative. Custom silicon “not only gives you a differentiation factor where you can be cheaper than competitors, but it also allows you to have some leverage over NVIDIA in negotiations.”\nChina data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and Colette Kress (Nvidia’s CFO) said losing that market, which NVIDIA sizes at “close to about $50 billion in the future,” would be material. Supply commitments of $119 billion compound demand risk if hyperscaler orders slow, and insiders have logged 16 recent transactions, net selling.\nNeither thesis has resolved. Blackwell ramps while Trainium and TPU volumes rise in parallel. NVIDIA’s NVLink Fusion strategy lets hyperscalers bolt custom accelerators onto NVIDIA’s fabric so the interconnect stays sticky even when compute does not.\nWatch hyperscaler capex mix, whether networking growth stays vertical, and any China SKU announcement over the next two quarters. Any one breaking hard could tip the call.\nNVIDIA trades at $196 against an analyst consensus target of ~$301.62 as of this writing, implying 53% upside. Coverage skews heavily positive with 10 Strong Buy, 48 Buy, 2 Hold, 1 Sell ratings. Forward P/E sits a…",
  "statement": "Supply commitments of $119 billion compound demand risk if hyperscaler orders slow"
 },
 {
