 {
  "id": "G066",
  "article_id": "gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0",
  "title": "Nvidia acts as backstop for customer GPUs in return for cut of cloud revenue",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "By Georgia Butler\nNvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\nFirmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, while Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\nIn a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said:",
  "passage": "By Georgia Butler\nNvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\nFirmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, while Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\nIn a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said: \"This new model enables AI clouds to procure Nvidia infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise, and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\n\"Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell Nvidia-powered cloud services, with Nvidia earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of Nvidia platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides Nvidia with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\"\nThis is not the first time Nvidia has sign…",
  "statement": "Firmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia"
 },
 {
  "id": "G442",
  "article_id": "gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c",
  "title": "TD Cowen Names Nvidia Stock a Top Pick on Massive Unpriced AI Upside",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-10",
  "article_opening": "Price change for Nvidia stock in last 1 year: 24%\n$NVDA Stock Price as of Jul. 9: $203\n52-Week High: $236\n$NVDA Stock Price Target: $302\nTD Cowen reiterated its Buy rating on Nvidia ( NVDA ) stock and named it a Top Pick, maintaining a $275 price target implying roughly 35% upside from where shares closed.\nThe call came after the firm hosted an investor lunch with Nvidia’s leadership team, including CEO Jensen Huang, CFO Colette Kress, and Head of Investor Relations Toshiya Hari.\nAnalyst Joshua ",
  "passage": "…h is part of Buchalter’s point about Nvidia’s opportunity being underappreciated.\nTD Cowen also highlighted evidence that computing capacity is genuinely tight right now. Rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices.\nAll of that points to demand outpacing supply.\nNvidia’s own recent quarterly results back up the strength story.\nThe company posted $82 billion in total revenue, up 85% year-over-year, marking its third straight quarter of accelerating growth.\nData center revenue alone hit $75 billion, up 92% year over year.\nNvidia also highlighted a new growth driver: its Vera CPU line, which the company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year, a notable push into a market historically dominated by Intel and AMD.\nBeyond the CPU expansion, Nvidia has been striking new partnerships, including a deal with Valar Atomics to build a nuclear-powered data center in Utah aimed at reducing water use for AI computing.\nAI startup Perplexity has also chosen to use Nvidia’s new CPUs for AI agent tasks.\nSee analysts’ growth forecasts and price targets for Nvidia stock (It’s free) >>>\nWhat the Market Is Telling Us About Nvidia Stock\nTD Cowen’s bullish stance reflects growing confidence that AI spending is durable, not a temporary spike, and that it’s spreading across more customers and use cases than investors currently give Nvidia credit for.\nS…",
  "statement": "its Vera CPU line, which the company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year"
 },
 {
  "id": "G154",
  "article_id": "gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568",
  "title": "Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-04",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) investors have had it good for several years. In 2023, 2024, and 2025, it produced market-crushing returns, making it one of the best stocks to own during each of those years. However, 2026 hasn't been that way. The stock is up 5% so far in 2026, which isn't nothing, but compared to the returns that Nvidia investors have enjoyed in recent years, it feels like an absolute failure. On another note, its market-crushing status is in jeopardy for 2026, as the S&P 500 is up nearl",
  "passage": "…g in clients once they've chosen to use its products.\nThis bodes well for the future, as AI hyperscalers are spending a massive amount of money this year and beyond to continue building out their AI computing footprints. The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion. That means more growth is coming to Nvidia, and the investing story is far from complete. Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia.\nHowever, the stock price doesn't reflect that.\nNvidia trades at 21.7 times forward earnings, essentially the same price tag as the S&P 500. This conveys that the market believes Nvidia will be a market-average stock after 2026's growth is accounted for. However, Wall Street analysts don't believe that, as they're guiding for major growth next year. That's why the stock looks so cheap when next year's earnings are used to value the stock.\nAs a result, I think Nvidia is an excellent stock to buy now. None of next year's growth has been priced in, which could lead to strong returns toward the end of this year.",
  "statement": "Nvidia trades at 21.7 times forward earnings, essentially the same price tag as the S&P 500"
 },
 {
  "id": "G436",
  "article_id": "gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e",
  "title": "DeepSeek Just Dealt Nvidia Stock a Potentially Major Blow",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-09",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek has already rattled Nvidia Corporation (NVDA) investors once with its low-cost artificial intelligence (AI) models. Now, the Chinese startup may be posing a far more direct competitive threat. Reports that DeepSeek is developing its own inference-focused AI chip could reduce its reliance on Nvidia's GPUs, highlighting a broader industry trend in which leading AI developers are increasingly designing custom silicon to lower costs, improve efficiency, and gain greater control over their A",
  "passage": "…vorable AI product mix.\nMoreover, the Data Center segment remained Nvidia's biggest growth driver, with revenue surging 92% YOY to a record $75.2 billion. Data Center networking revenue soared 199% YOY to $14.8 billion, while computing revenue accounted for the remaining $60.4 billion.\nIts Edge Computing revenue increased 29% YOY to $6.4 billion, supported by demand across gaming GPUs, autonomous driving, robotics, and AI-enabled edge devices.\nManagement also pointed to accelerating adoption of Blackwell systems and highlighted expanding opportunities in agentic AI and enterprise AI infrastructure.\nAdditionally, Nvidia issued another optimistic outlook for the second quarter of fiscal 2027, forecasting revenue of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%, and a non-GAAP gross margin of about 75%. The guidance assumes no contribution from China Data Center compute revenue due to ongoing U.S. export restrictions.\nStreet expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 90.2% YOY to $8.69 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 34.3% increase to $11.67 in fiscal 2028.\nLast month, Morgan Stanley reaffirmed its \"Overweight\" rating on the stock and maintained its $288 price target, reiterating Nvidia as its top pick in the processor space.\nPlus, Truist Securities reiterated its \"Buy\" rating and $307 price target after Nvidia unveiled several next-generation AI products at GTC Taipei.\nOverall, NVDA has a consensus \"Strong Buy\" rating. Of the 49 analysts covering t…",
  "statement": "forecasting revenue of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%, and a non-GAAP gross margin of about 75%"
 },
 {
  "id": "G195",
  "article_id": "gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d",
  "title": "Nvidia pushes Kyber release to 2028 following manufacturing concerns – report",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "By Charlotte Trueman\nNvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028, SemiAnalysis has reported.\nThe Kyber server cabinets, which have been designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via the company’s NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027.\nKyber allows GPUs to be mounted in compute trays that sit vertically rather than horizontally, a design that Nvidia has described as being “like books o",
  "passage": "…against its “odd design and heavy operational burden.”\nThe manufacturing issues will also impact the planned NVL576 system, which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches, meaning that the system will also likely be delayed or limited to small volumes, the firm noted. All this leaves Nvidia with “no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,” SemiAnalysis said.\nFirst announced at Nvidia’s GTC conference in 2025, Nvidia has previously said the liquid-cooled Kyber racks will offer a 4x performance improvement over its Blackwell NVL72 system – also known as Oberon – and consume some 600kW.\nThe news comes a week after SemiAnalysis separately reported that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of the two-chiplet design, also due to “manufacturing execution concerns.” The two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\nIn response to a request for comment from DCD regarding the Kyber claims, a spokesperson for Nvidia said: \"Our roadmap is intact.\"",
  "statement": "Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of the two-chiplet design"
 },
 {
  "id": "G531",
  "article_id": "gnews:dbff79c9778e4b3d8ac8eef2",
  "title": "Nokia advances AI-RAN toward commercial deployment and physical AI, launches sovereign agentic AI platform for telco autonomy with NVIDIA",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-21",
  "article_opening": "Nokia advances AI-RAN toward commercial deployment and physical AI, launches sovereign agentic AI platform for telco autonomy with NVIDIA\nNew capabilities improve radio performance, enable real-time intelligence at the edge and extend governed AI agents across network operations.\nNokia AI-native anyRAN software and NVIDIA accelerated computing turn the radio access network into a distributed platform for sensing, intelligence and real-time action.\nBT, Deutsche Telekom, Elisa and Orange provide g",
  "passage": "…ross network operations. This creates a trusted, software-defined foundation that helps operators get more from their spectrum today while enabling new Physical AI use cases and opening a practical path toward increasingly autonomous, AI-native 6G networks.\nThe next generation of telecommunications infrastructure will be built on a software-defined architecture. NVIDIA and Nokia are combining CUDA accelerated computing and AI with Nokia’s network software and expertise to improve radio performance and deploy trusted AI agents. Together, we are advancing software-defined, AI-native 5G and laying the foundation for 6G.\nRecent customer activity provides growing evidence of deployment progress:\nIn Helsinki, Elisa ran voice, mobile data and video analytics simultaneously on the same AI-RAN platform in its 5G network, demonstrating how AI-RAN can support radio and AI workloads in a live network environment. Distributed deployment and the evaluation of customer use cases and commercial AI-RAN services are expected in 2027.\nBT is preparing to deploy a live-network trial in Birmingham in 2027.\nDeutsche Telekom and Nokia are developing AI-based channel estimation as part of a broader set of AI-driven capabilities to improve network performance and efficiency. While this is just one element of the broader AI-RAN opportunity, initial laboratory measurements showed typical uplink throughput improvements of 10–15%, with greater gains under more complex radio conditions. The next phase will extend this work with…",
  "statement": "In Helsinki, Elisa ran voice, mobile data and video analytics simultaneously on the same AI-RAN platform in its 5G network"
 },
 {
